================================================================
Third-party code, datasets, and references
================================================================

1. CleverHans (TensorFlow 2 and PyTorch attack implementations)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   Vendored under:
     attacks/cleverhans_tf2_copy/     (FGSM, BIM, PGD, CW-L2, SPSA, Madry, ...)
     attacks/cleverhans_torch_copy/   (FGSM, BIM, PGD, CW-L2, MI / NI / 1D-TI-MI FGSM)
   Original project: https://github.com/cleverhans-lab/cleverhans
   License: MIT
   Notes:
     - Subsets of the CleverHans attacks are vendored with minor adaptations
       for 2D pen-trajectory inputs in the range [-1, 1].
     - Original copyright notices are retained inside each vendored file.
     - The flat re-export files at attacks/{fgsm,bim,pgd,cw_l2,...}_tf.py and
       attacks/{fgsm,bim,pgd,cw_l2,mi_fgsm,ni_fgsm,ti_mi_fgsm_1d}_torch.py
       only re-export public symbols; they do not modify algorithm logic.

2. Grad-CAM++ (adapted for 1D Conv on pen trajectories)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   Adaptation under:
     xai/gradcam/gradcam_unipen.py                       (legacy/xai/, retained)
     attacks/tamura_method/aite.py                       (signed Grad-CAM)
     attacks/tamura_method/aite_casia_cnn1d.py           (PyTorch port)
   Reference: Chattopadhyay et al., "Grad-CAM++: Generalized Gradient-based
              Visual Explanations for Deep Convolutional Networks", WACV 2018.
   Notes:
     - The "signed" variant used by AITE removes the trailing ReLU so that
       negative contributions are kept.
     - Adapted to operate on Conv1D feature maps over pen trajectories.

3. LIME (adapted for time-series segments)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   Adaptation under:
     xai/lime/lime_unipen.py                              (legacy/xai/, retained)
   Original project: https://github.com/marcotcr/lime
   License: BSD-2-Clause
   Notes:
     - Segment-based perturbation strategy was adapted for 2D pen
       trajectories. LIME-based attack scripts are retained in legacy/ for
       reference and are not part of the paper's reported results.

4. MI-FGSM / NI-FGSM / 1D-TI-MI-FGSM (transfer-based black-box baselines)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   References:
     - MI-FGSM       : Dong et al., "Boosting Adversarial Attacks with
                       Momentum", CVPR 2018.
     - NI-FGSM       : Lin et al., "Nesterov Accelerated Gradient and Scale
                       Invariance for Adversarial Attacks", ICLR 2020.
     - 1D-TI-MI-FGSM : Wang et al. (2022) 1D adaptation of the
                       Translation-Invariant MI-FGSM for time-series.
   Notes:
     - The TensorFlow stand-alone implementations under
       attacks/blackbox_attack/One-shot transfer black-box attacks/ are
       original to this work.
     - The PyTorch counterparts are vendored from CleverHans-derived code
       under attacks/cleverhans_torch_copy/ and obey the upstream MIT terms.

5. Datasets (NOT redistributed in this repository)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   - UNIPEN
       https://www.unipen.org/
       Obtained from the Unipen consortium; subject to the consortium's
       terms of use.
   - CASIA-OLHWDB
       http://www.nlpr.ia.ac.cn/databases/handwriting/Home.html
       Obtained from the CASIA web page; subject to CASIA's research-use
       license.

   Please consult the original licensors for terms of use. The repository
   only contains placeholder directories (data/unipen/, data/casia/) and
   loader code; the raw data files are not included.

6. Temporal smoothness metric
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   Reference: Pialla et al., "Smoothness Metrics for Adversarial Time-Series
              Generation", 2023.
   Implementation: experiments/smoothness.py (mean second-order finite
              difference).
